Naming Conventions
Variables & Functions: Use camelCase. For example, userName, getUserData.

Classes & Interfaces: Use PascalCase, such as UserService, UserInterface.

Files & Folders: Use either kebab-case or camelCase, and maintain consistency across the project, e.g., user-service.ts or userService.ts.

Constants: Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE, like API_URL, MAX_COUNT.

Project Structure & File Rules
Import Order: Sort imports by external libraries, internal modules, and styles, typically in alphabetical order and grouped logically.

Extensions: Use .ts or .tsx for TypeScript (especially React components), and .js or .jsx for JavaScript.

File Naming: Use lowercase and hyphens or camelCase consistently. Maintain a consistent folder structure by feature or domain.

Documentation & Comments
JSDoc: Use JSDoc for functions, classes, and interfaces to describe purpose, inputs, outputs, and exceptions.

Comments: Write clear, concise comments for complex logic. Keep comments updated with code changes.

CI/CD Integration
Lint & Format: Integrate linting and formatting into CI/CD pipelines to automatically validate all commits.

JavaScript/TypeScript Practices
Functions: Use arrow functions for simple logic. Clearly distinguish between declarations and expressions.

This Binding: Be cautious with arrow functions in class methods, as they inherit the parent context’s this.

Immutability: Use const for non-reassignable values, and let only when reassignment is necessary.

Scope: Use block-scoped variable declarations and minimize global variables.

Explicit Types: Prefer explicit type annotations in TypeScript for better readability and stability.

Interface vs. Type: Use interface for object shapes, and type for complex or union types.

Generics: Use generics to ensure type safety in reusable functions and components.

Props Typing: Always use type aliases to define function and component props.

ES6+ Features
Destructuring: Use destructuring to keep code concise when extracting values from objects/arrays.

Spread Operator: Use the spread operator to copy and update arrays or objects while preserving immutability.

Async/Await: Use async/await for readability in asynchronous code.

Error Handling: Use try/catch in async functions, and .catch() with Promises.

Error Messages: Provide clear and informative messages for both users and developers.

Error & Exception Management
Global Handlers: Use a global error handler in backend frameworks (e.g., Express, NestJS) for consistent exception handling.

Code Quality
Pure Functions: Write pure functions with predictable outputs to improve testability and reusability.

Modularity: Organize code by feature or responsibility to improve maintainability.

Dependency Injection: Use DI in backend frameworks to reduce coupling between modules.

DRY Principle: Avoid code duplication by extracting shared logic into utilities or modules.

KISS Principle: Keep logic simple and implement only what is necessary.

YAGNI Principle: Avoid over-engineering and do not add features until they are needed.

Performance: Avoid unnecessary re-renders, memory leaks, and optimize network requests.

Input Validation: Thoroughly validate all user inputs to prevent security vulnerabilities (e.g., SQL injection, XSS).

Environment Variables: Use .env files for sensitive configs and exclude them from version control.

Dependency Management: Regularly check for security updates and replace vulnerable packages promptly.